## Comments inside JSX

JSX has no comment syntax of its own. Inside markup you open a JavaScript expression with braces and put a block comment in it:

```jsx
function Greeting() {
  return (
    <div>
      {/* this is a JSX comment */}
      <h1>Hello</h1>
      {/* comments can
          span multiple lines too */}
    </div>
  );
}
```

The `{}` is a normal expression slot, and a block comment evaluates to nothing, so React renders nothing for it.

## Why a plain // does not work

A `//` line comment placed directly in markup is treated as text and rendered on the page. Even inside braces it is risky, because the closing `}` can end up commented out — keep comments to the block form in JSX.

## Comments in component logic

Outside the returned JSX — the function body, hooks, handlers — it is ordinary JavaScript, so normal `//` and block comments work as usual:

```jsx
function Counter() {
  // plain JS comment — fine here
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
  return <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>{count}</button>;
}
```

## Comment with intent

The best comment explains why, not what — the markup already says what. A note on why a workaround exists, or why an element is conditionally hidden, is worth far more than one restating the tag.

## What it is

The virtual DOM is a tree of plain JavaScript objects that mirrors the structure of the real DOM. Each node records a type, props, and children — cheap to create and cheap to discard, unlike real DOM nodes.

## The update cycle

When a component renders, React produces a new virtual tree. It compares that tree against the previous one — diffing — to work out exactly what changed, then commits only those differences to the real DOM:

```jsx
function Counter() {
  const [n, setN] = useState(0);
  return <p>Count: {n}</p>;
}
// setN(1) -> new virtual tree -> diff finds only the text changed
// -> React updates just that one text node
```

## Why the indirection helps

Real DOM operations are expensive — layout, reflow, repaint — and mutating the DOM by hand is error-prone. The virtual DOM lets you re-describe the whole UI on every state change as if it were cheap, and React derives the minimal real update for you.

## The honest caveat

The virtual DOM is not magically fast — it trades some diffing work for a much simpler programming model. Frameworks like Svelte skip it by compiling updates ahead of time. For React, the real performance lever is rendering less: stable keys, memoisation, and not putting derivable data in state.
